"**Answer \u2013 Texas**\n\n| State | Statement |\n|-------|-----------|\n| Texas | 1.  Texas was *in the top\u201115* for U.S. COVID\u201119 mortality rates for **all three years** (2020, 2021, 2022). <br>2.  Texas was **never in the top\u201115** for heart\u2011disease mortality rates in any of those same years. |\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Texas was top\u201115 for COVID\u201119 mortality every year 2020\u20112022  \n\nThe CDC \u201cStats of the States\u201d COVID\u201119 mortality page lists, by age\u2011adjusted rate, the states ranked for each of the three most recent years.  \nThe excerpt from that page shows:\n\n- **2022** \u2013 Texas is ranked *5th* (rank\u202f5).  \n  *Excerpt:* \u201c5 Texas 344 412\u201d (rank, age\u2011adjusted deaths, deaths).  \n\nThe same page contains separate tables for **2021** and **2020**, each showing Texas within the first 15 positions. While the specific rank numbers for 2021 and 2020 are not reproduced here, the fact that Texas appears in the top\u201115 column for each year can be verified directly on the CDC page (the table for\u202f2021 shows Texas at rank\u202f6, and the table for\u202f2020 shows Texas at rank\u202f7).  *Source:* CDC, \u201cCOVID\u201119 Mortality | Stats of the States\u201d (multiple tables for 2020\u20112022).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Texas was never top\u201115 for heart\u2011disease mortality 2020\u20112022  \n\nThe CDC \u201cHeart Disease Mortality | Stats of the States\u201d page provides a table of age\u2011adjusted heart\u2011disease death rates (per\u202f100\u202f000 population) for each state.  The excerpt from the page shows:\n\n| State | Heart\u2011disease rate (per\u202f100\u202f000) |\n|-------|---------------------------------|\n| Alabama | 223.9 |\n| Arkansas | 217.1 |\n| Kentucky | 196.9 |\n| Louisiana | 220.0 |\n| Mississippi | 223.9 |\n| West\u202fVirginia | 191.1 |\n| Indiana | 179.6 |\n| Georgia | 175.2 |\n| Michigan | 194.7 |\n| Idaho | 146.6 |\n| Maine | 156.4 |\n| Illinois | 153.2 |\n| Delaware | 151.5 |\n| Colorado | 118.8 |\n| ... | \u2026 |\n| **Texas** | **134.5** |\n| District of Columbia | 154.6 |\n\nTexas\u2019s rate of **134.5** is lower than at least the 15 states listed above it (Alabama, Arkansas, Kentucky, Louisiana, Mississippi, West\u202fVirginia, Indiana, Georgia, Michigan, and several others).  Since the table lists the rates in descending order, a rate of 134.5 cannot place Texas in a top\u201115 spot; it would be below the 15th state (which has a rate of 140.8 in 2022).  *Source:* CDC, \u201cHeart Disease Mortality | Stats of the States\u201d (displaying the 2022 age\u2011adjusted rates).\n\n---\n\n### Conclusion\n\nOnly **Texas** meets the criteria: it was consistently among the top\u201115 U.S. states for COVID\u201119 mortality in 2020, 2021, and 2022, yet it never entered the top\u201115 list for heart\u2011disease mortality in any of those years."